{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788291411,"nonce":"83wSFELOg5EPUz+3HFAAu0jOIxw=","message":"CLOSED by session 19, on branch `spec/until-condition` (commit 477dd64), proven\nlive on `_RimWorld-Agent --quicktest`.\n\n## Acceptance, item by item\n\n- **\"answers for exactly that layout and no others, proven with TWO layouts in\n  flight\"** — done with THREE, because two was not enough to settle the second\n  bullet. Phase 1 of `accept/fc287ba-until-state.py` places one layout in\n  instant mode (four elements, resolved on return), one in blueprint mode which\n  it then cancels, and one it leaves live. All three reads differ, and the live\n  layout's blueprint count is a strict subset of the whole-map read while the\n  BUILT layout reports zero of its own — which is the check that would have\n  caught the original defect, since answering whole-map was right only by luck.\n- **\"distinguishes the states a caller acts on\"** — `by_state` speaks one\n  six-token vocabulary: the four live states from `ConstructionVerbs.State`\n  (blocked \u003e in-progress \u003e awaiting-materials \u003e ready, precedence unchanged)\n  plus `built` and `cancelled` borrowed from `Placements` rather than\n  redeclared. A seventh token, `unscanned`, means \"not measured\" — a live\n  element past `scan_cap` gets no `Frame.WorkToBuild` and no cost list, so\n  reporting it as `ready` would be a measurement the call did not make.\n- **\"a fully built layout is distinguishable from a fully cancelled one\"** —\n  1.5b/1.6b: `built: 4, cancelled: 0` against `built: 0, cancelled: 4`, both\n  `done: true`. Both are empty ground and a rect-scoped read cannot tell them\n  apart at all.\n- **\"`rect_source` names `layout`\"** — 1.4n.\n- **\"an unknown `layout_id` is a refusal naming the ids this session knows\"** —\n  0.5a/0.5b.\n- **\"`layout_id` together with `rect`/`id`/`placement_id` is a refusal, not a\n  precedence rule\"** — 0.5c. The sentence says why: a layout's elements are not\n  the same set as its rect, so there is no precedence rule that would not be a\n  guess. `NearMiss` also refuses the spellings `layout`, `layoutid`, `ly_id`\n  (0.5d).\n- **\"the layout read does not re-enter a lazy getter per placement\"** — the\n  rollup is `Layouts.Progress`, one `thingGrid.ThingsListAtFast` per element and\n  no allocation past the result object; `WorkerIndex` is built once per call, as\n  the rect path builds it. Measured in its other role as the halting predicate:\n  0.0131 ms per evaluation for a 22-element layout, 0.0008 ms/frame.\n- **\"zero red errors\"** — 5.5, across the whole 140-check run.\n\n## Two things the fix turned out to need that the issue did not ask for\n\n**`Placements.StateOf`**, `Answer`'s live look with the dictionary removed.\n`Answer` allocates fourteen keys and two strings, and `fc287ba` polls every\nelement of a layout once per cadence window. `Answer` now calls it, so the read\nand the halt cannot drift into disagreeing about what a placement id points at.\n\n**`LayoutProgress.Missing`.** A placement id this record still names that the\nplacement table no longer holds — reachable past `Placements`' 2000-entry cap.\nIt counts as RESOLVED, deliberately, because the alternative is an advance that\ncan never halt on a layout whose evidence has been discarded, and \"halted with\n`unknown: 3` in the report\" beats a timeout that says nothing. It ships without\na witness and is named on `039e359`.\n\n## What this closed for the next issue\n\n`fc287ba`'s \"Why it matters beyond convenience\" was right: a predicate can only\naddress what an observer can scope. `advance {until:{layout:\"ly-N\"}}` halts on\n`Layouts.Progress`, the same uncapped rollup this verb publishes — which is why\nonly per-element DETAIL is capped here and the headline counts never are.","files":null}]}